Modifications made in this fork (github.com/kelnei/rewrite-body), relative to
upstream v1.2.0 (commit a37d560, 24 November 2022):

  * go.mod, all Go import paths, and .traefik.yml were updated from
    github.com/packruler/rewrite-body to github.com/kelnei/rewrite-body so the
    module path matches this repository.

  * logger/logger.go: the Log*f methods now check the configured log level
    before formatting the message. Previously fmt.Sprintf was passed as an
    argument to writeLog and therefore evaluated unconditionally, so discarded
    log lines still built their message in full. Because the handler logs the
    whole response body twice per request, this allocated roughly twice the
    page size on every request even with logging disabled.

  * logger/logger.go: the log prefix now ends with a separator. log.Ldate writes
    the date immediately after the prefix, so every line previously ran the level
    into the timestamp as "Rewrite-Body | DEBUG2026/07/24 20:31:41".

  * logger/logger_format_test.go was added to cover both of the above.

  * handler/handler.go: ServeHTTP no longer recovers panics from the handler it
    wraps. Upstream recovered every one, logged it and returned, so the server
    completed the response with whatever had been written -- verified against
    Traefik 3.7.16, where a panic below the plugin, http.ErrAbortHandler
    included, reached the client as a 200 with Content-Length: 0 and any cache
    headers the handler had set, instead of a 500 or a closed connection. The
    panic now passes up to Traefik's own recovery, so a route behaves the same
    with the plugin as without it.

  * httputil/request_wrapper.go: a request is skipped as a WebSocket upgrade
    only when its Connection header lists upgrade and its Upgrade header lists
    websocket, each read as a case-insensitive, comma-separated list across all
    header lines. Upstream matched websocket case-sensitively in the first
    Upgrade line and never read Connection, so a plain request that carried an
    Upgrade header was skipped and its page left unrewritten.

  * httputil/vary.go was added, and handler/handler.go,
    httputil/request_wrapper.go and httputil/response_writer.go changed to use
    it: a rewritten response carries Vary: Accept, as does a request skipped on
    its Accept header, so a shared cache can tell the rewritten and unrewritten
    copies of one URL apart. A forwarded response does not get it, since its
    bytes do not depend on the request.

  * handler/handler.go: full response bodies are logged at Trace rather than
    Debug, leaving Debug usable as an operator log level.

  * handler/handler.go and httputil/response_writer.go: the Debug lines that
    described a request or a failed response no longer print the whole
    *http.Request or http.Header. Those carry Authorization, Cookie, Set-Cookie,
    the query string and the client address, so running at Debug wrote
    credentials to the log. The lines now name the method, host, path and the
    few headers that decide how the plugin treats the exchange.

  * httputil/request_wrapper.go: the request Accept header no longer has to name
    a monitored content type. */*, a range wildcard and a missing header are all
    treated as open-ended and fall through to the response Content-Type check,
    so fetch and XMLHttpRequest responses are rewritten.

  * httputil/response_writer.go: only a response the plugin can actually rewrite
    is buffered. The rest are forwarded to the client write by write, with their
    headers untouched, rather than being held in memory to be handed straight
    back at the end of the request. This is what makes the Accept change above
    safe: an open-ended request that turns out to be a download or a stream no
    longer costs its whole length in memory, keeps the Content-Length and
    Last-Modified the backend set, and reaches the client as it arrives.
    Measured against Traefik 3.7.9: a 24 kB chunked response delivered over
    0.9s previously arrived in one piece at the end.

  * httputil/request_wrapper.go: monitored methods are compared exactly rather
    than by substring, and HEAD is never processed -- a HEAD response has no
    body to rewrite, and buffering one cost the response its Content-Length.

  * httputil/response_writer.go: GetContent decodes from a copy of the buffered
    response, so a failed decode no longer consumes part of the body that the
    error path then writes to the client. SetContent no longer discards the
    re-encode error, which used to leave an empty body under a success status.
    Unwrap was added for http.ResponseController, and CloseNotify falls back to
    the request context instead of a channel nothing can ever send on.

  * compressutil/compressutil.go: Content-Encoding: deflate is encoded as zlib
    (RFC 1950) as HTTP specifies, having previously been raw DEFLATE that only
    this plugin could read back. Decoding accepts either form. ReaderError is
    now unwrappable.

  * httputil/monitoring_config.go: EnsureProperFormat, which undid a 2022 config
    parser quirk that mangled string lists, was removed after confirming
    Traefik 3.7.9 delivers proper slices through both the file provider and
    docker labels.

  * .traefik.yml: the testData block named `monitor` where the configuration key
    is `monitoring`, so it never bound. An unknown key is dropped in silence and
    the defaults backfilled values identical to the ones written there, which is
    what hid it.

  * README.md: the static configuration example dropped the `pilot` block, which
    has had no meaning since Traefik Pilot shut down. The description of which
    responses get processed said the Content-Type had to include "text" and gave
    text/json as an example; it is matched against the configured monitoring
    types, which default to text/html alone. What each of the three plugins in
    this lineage contributes is now stated where the reader starts, rather than
    the fork's own additions being described as the original's.

  * .github/workflows and .golangci.yaml were updated so lint and tests -- both
    compiled and under Yaegi -- actually run for this fork, on every supported Go
    release. The workflow's actions are pinned to commit SHAs, and its two tools
    are installed from release tarballs checked against a recorded SHA-256.
    .github/dependabot.yml bumps the pinned actions. Tests were added
    throughout for the above.

  * docs/TRUST_BOUNDARIES.md was added. It records who can reach the plugin,
    what each log level writes and what is deliberately not a boundary.

  * SECURITY.md was added. It says how to report a vulnerability privately and
    which release is maintained.

  * .devcontainer was removed. It described upstream's own workstation -- Go
    1.18, Yaegi v0.9.20, a deprecated base image, and bind mounts of shell
    dotfiles -- and could not be created anywhere those files are missing.

  * .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE was removed. Issues are disabled on this repository,
    so the templates could never be offered.

  * Makefile: the vendor and clean targets were removed, since the plugin has
    no dependencies to vendor, along with GO111MODULE=on, which has been Go's
    default since 1.16. .PHONY now names the targets the Makefile has.
